From 1c97cad8c5f5c9ab2738a57e6630f843aada17fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 12:59:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] html: give <iframe> the HTML default object size of 300x150

An <iframe> with no CSS width/height had no size at all in the UA sheet, so it collapsed
to its 2px border: neither the frame box nor the document inside it rendered, and every
such test failed as MissingContent even though the sub-document projection itself works.

HTML treats an iframe as a replaced element with a default object size of 300x150 when
nothing else supplies one. Expressing that as a UA width/height is an approximation of
the sizing algorithm -- an author `width: auto` wins over a UA declaration and would
still shrink the box, where a real default object size would keep it 300 wide -- but it
covers the case that matters and matches how the rule already models the iframe border.

Deliberately not <frame>: it only ever appears inside a <frameset>, which sizes and
positions its own cells (LayoutFramesetChildren), so a fixed default object size and
inline-block display would fight that grid instead of helping it.

WPT resource-timing goes 91 -> 92 of 127 with no regressions, including
tentative/initiator-url/static-resource (item 28 of the biggest-problems report), whose
iframe now renders green.html at 300x150 as Chromium's golden does.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 Source/Broiler.HTML.Core/CssDefaults.cs | 15 +++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Source/Broiler.HTML.Core/CssDefaults.cs b/Source/Broiler.HTML.Core/CssDefaults.cs
index f2ac049..10abce4 100644
--- a/Source/Broiler.HTML.Core/CssDefaults.cs
+++ b/Source/Broiler.HTML.Core/CssDefaults.cs
@@ -116,8 +116,19 @@ internal static class CssDefaults
            the author shorthand, so the legacy `<table border>` grey is applied
            directly in DomParser (TranslateAttributes / ApplyTableBorder), not
            here, and author CSS is left to win normally. */
-        /* Replaced inline elements — WHATWG default rendering */
-        iframe          { border: 2px inset; display: inline-block }
+        /* Replaced inline elements — WHATWG default rendering.
+           The 300x150 is the HTML replaced-elements rule: an iframe is a replaced element
+           with a *default object size* of 300x150, used when nothing else gives it one.
+           Expressing that as a UA width/height is an approximation of the sizing algorithm
+           (an author `width: auto` would win here and shrink the box, where a real default
+           object size would still be 300 wide), but it is the case that matters: without it
+           an unsized <iframe> collapsed to its border and neither the frame nor its document
+           rendered at all — every such test failed as MissingContent
+           (WPT resource-timing/tentative/initiator-url/static-resource and friends).
+           Deliberately *not* <frame>: it only ever appears inside a <frameset>, which sizes
+           and positions its cells itself (DomParser.LayoutFramesetChildren), so a default
+           object size would fight the grid rather than help it. */
+        iframe          { border: 2px inset; display: inline-block; width: 300px; height: 150px }
         object          { display: inline-block }
 
         /* HTML5 semantic/sectioning elements – display:block per WHATWG */
